tools.description:
Execute commands in a persistent PTY shell session with full interactive terminal support.

## When to use Terminal vs Bash
- **Bash**: Non-interactive commands (ls, grep, git, pip install, pytest). Returns instantly when done. Preferred for most work.
- **Terminal**: Interactive programs (vim, pdb, gdb, ssh, mysql), TUI apps, long-running servers you want to monitor, or anything requiring a real PTY. Sessions run in tmux with a real pseudo-terminal.

## Session model
Each shell_id is an independent persistent tmux session. Working directory, environment variables, and running processes survive across calls. Use different shell_ids for parallel activities (e.g. "editor" for vim, "debug" for pdb, "server" for a web server).

## command vs keystrokes
- **command** (string): Shell command to execute. Enter is auto-appended. Use this to start programs (vim, gdb, python, ssh) or run one-shot commands.
- **keystrokes** (string): Raw keystrokes sent verbatim to an already-running interactive program. Use \n for Enter, \t for Tab. No auto-Enter — you control exactly what is sent. Special keys (C-c, C-d, Escape, Up, Down, etc.) must be sent as their own separate call, e.g. {"keystrokes": "C-c"}.

## wait_for strategies
- Omit wait_for for fire-and-check: command auto-yields after ~10s; call again with empty command to poll.
- `"timeout"` with short duration (0.5-2s) — best for interactive apps (vim, pdb) where the program stays open.
- `"timeout"` with longer duration (10-30s) — for builds/scripts you want to wait on.

## result_type
- `"auto"` (default) — returns the full tmux scrollback (up to ~10000 lines) with a client-side cursor, so subsequent polls on the same shell_id return only new output since last read. Best for streaming / long-running commands.
- `"full_history"` — always returns full scrollback without cursor; use when you want the complete history every call.
- `"capture_pane"` — visible terminal screen only (40 rows). Use for TUI apps (vim/pdb/top) where the visible frame is what you want.

## Examples

1. Open vim and edit a file:
   {"shell_id": "editor", "command": "vim /tmp/test.py", "wait_for": {"type": "timeout", "timeout": 1}, "result_type": "capture_pane"}
   {"shell_id": "editor", "keystrokes": ":set paste\ni\ndef hello():\n    print('hello')\n\x1b:wq\n", "wait_for": {"type": "timeout", "timeout": 1}, "result_type": "capture_pane"}

2. Debug with pdb:
   {"shell_id": "debug", "command": "python -m pdb script.py", "wait_for": {"type": "timeout", "timeout": 2}, "result_type": "capture_pane"}
   {"shell_id": "debug", "keystrokes": "n\n", "wait_for": {"type": "timeout", "timeout": 1}, "result_type": "capture_pane"}
   {"shell_id": "debug", "keystrokes": "p result\n", "wait_for": {"type": "timeout", "timeout": 1}, "result_type": "capture_pane"}

3. Start a server and monitor:
   {"shell_id": "server", "command": "python -m http.server 8080", "wait_for": {"type": "timeout", "timeout": 2}}
   {"shell_id": "server", "wait_for": {"type": "timeout", "timeout": 1}}
   {"shell_id": "server", "keystrokes": "C-c", "wait_for": {"type": "timeout", "timeout": 1}}

4. Wait for test suite to finish: {"command": "pytest -x", "wait_for": {"type": "timeout", "timeout": 30}}

5. SSH session: {"shell_id": "remote", "command": "ssh user@host", "wait_for": {"type": "timeout", "timeout": 5}, "result_type": "capture_pane"}

Launch specialized sub-agents for complex work that benefits from parallelism, specialization, isolation, or background execution.

For single-sub-agent plans, use this tool only when delegation adds context-specific value (coordination capacity, specialization, isolation, or long-running execution); otherwise handle simple, bounded work directly.

Available sub-agent types:
- Planner: Strategic planning specialist for complex tasks. **Prefer this when planning is needed**. Gathers necessary context through file exploration and web research, then designs structured plans. Supports both implementation and research planning. Strictly read-only — does not write/edit files. (Tools: Bash, Glob, Grep, Read, WebSearch, WebFetch)
- Explorer: Fast codebase exploration specialist. Use for targeted searches: finding files by patterns, searching code for keywords, or quickly understanding specific modules. **Prefer Planner for comprehensive exploration with plan design**. Read-only. (Tools: Glob, Grep, Read)
- GeneralPurpose: General-purpose agent for researching complex questions, searching for code, and executing multi-step tasks. When you are searching for a keyword or file and are not confident that you will find the right match in the first few tries use this agent to perform the search for you. (Tools: All tools)
- DeepResearch: Specialized agent for extensive online research and information gathering tasks. Use this agent when you need to conduct comprehensive research on topics, gather information from multiple sources, analyze trends, or answer complex questions that require browsing publicly available information. Can read local files (e.g. tool_output_guard persisted output). (Tools: WebSearch, WebFetch, Read)

Usage notes:
- Sub-agents run asynchronously; this call returns immediately with their agent IDs.
- Prefer launching multiple independent tasks in one Task call when orchestration is warranted.
- Prompts carry specs and decisions, not implementation artifacts the sub-agent could paste into files.
- When fork_from is set, the sub-agent already inherits that agent's conversation as background — state only the objective and what differs; do not restate inherited context.
- Use relative paths in prompts; sub-agents resolve them against their own working directory, which may differ from yours.

Example:
{{
  "description": "Fix the bug in authentication module",
  "tasks": [
    {{
      "subagent_type": "Planner",
      "subagent_name": "fix_attempt_1",
      "prompt": "Investigate and fix the authentication bug in ./src/auth/login.py"
    }},
    {{
      "subagent_type": "Explorer",
      "subagent_name": "fix_attempt_2",
      "prompt": "Find an alternative approach to fix the authentication bug"
    }}
  ]
}}
